1790:      "id": "phase-077",
1884:      "finding": "97 ```redblue blocks exist across the documents that define Redblue - SPEC.md 64, docs/GRAMMAR.md 9, PHILOSOPHY.md 9, HANDOUT.md 8, README.md 7 - and no test runs any of them. Every existing reference is a hand-quoted single example (tests/for_range_test.rs:2 cites SPEC.md:380-391, tests/formatter_test.rs:2008 cites SPEC.md:383). Measured this audit by extracting and running every block: 58 of 97 do not run, among them SPEC.md:319 and SPEC.md:327 (the phase-080 forms), SPEC.md:1068 (`LexerError: Unexpected character '\\''` - Redblue has no single-quoted string), SPEC.md:1066 (`ParserError: Unexpected token Wait`, owned by phase-049) and HANDOUT.md:155 (`filter was called with arguments it cannot use`, owned by phase-077). Many of the 58 are fragments rather than defects - docs/GRAMMAR.md:526-532's example omits its `end` - so the first deliverable is an honest classification, NOT 58 features. The rotted examples are exactly the ones that cost an audit to find: `items at 0` sat in SPEC.md undetected until now.",
2280:      "finding": "Redblue lists are values, not iterators, so every helper that is lazy in other languages is materialised here, and the ones a program actually needs are simply missing. There is no chain to join two sequences, no zip to walk two together, no enumerate to pair with an index, no product to cross two sequences, no groupby to bucket, and no memoisation at all - so a recursive function that recomputes the same subproblem has no way to avoid it. phase-077 adds sort, reverse, zip, enumerate, filter and reduce to the list surface; this phase is the functions that are not list methods, and it is scoped to avoid overlapping phase-077.",
